                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                        "entry_id": "c1d8c846-22ce-385b-8bc8-07ffd1b439b7",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A venomous spider, Latrodectus hasselti, endemic to Australia."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Australia"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bbf3d2d5-562c-41d3-a81c-ad3e7d87691d",
                                "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                                "text": "1988 June 16, Stephanie Pain, Things that go plop in the night, New Scientist, page 78,\n“If you find a redback, squash it firmly,” advised one guide."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "fead271f-a250-4a84-ae1d-5c2d6bbce84d",
                                "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                                "text": "Many a red-back, funnel-web, or other spider has found its resting days at the bottom of a vegemite jar shoved into some dark recess of the laundry."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "00b2a207-5a64-4e2b-9859-263ae643d35e",
                                "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                                "text": "2005 October, Martha Harrison, Brilliant 10: Marydianne Andrade, Popular Science, page 58,\nAndrade first encountered the redback— whose lethal bite gives it as infamous a reputation in Australia as the black widow enjoys here in North America—when her grad-school adviser asked to travel to go to Perth to study them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "6c9732e9-009d-46aa-a99f-cb03648f92d7",
                                "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                                "text": "Easily identified by a red, orange or brownish stripe on the abdomen, Redbacks are responsible for by far the most spider bites in Australia."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "29c8c95a-39f1-403e-a30a-77545bd2b4e3",
                                "sense_id": "fcf980ca-bed6-3103-8983-1bea366ed318",
                                "text": "The only truly dangerous Australian spiders are funnelwebs and redbacks but, these days, deaths of people from these spiders seem to be a thing of the past since antivenom is now available."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
